WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.899
Retrieval-based language models sharpen their
predictions by pulling examples from a huge

00:00:05.979 --> 00:00:07.869
external datastore at test time,

00:00:07.949 --> 00:00:12.732
but that nearest-neighbor search is slow and runs
at almost every single token.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:16.610
This paper introduces RetoMaton, short for
retrieval automaton,

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:20.611
which builds a weighted finite automaton on top of
the datastore.

00:00:20.691 --> 00:00:25.659
By saving pointers between consecutive entries and
clustering entries into states,

00:00:25.739 --> 00:00:31.446
RetoMaton lets the model follow cheap automaton
transitions instead of searching from scratch.

00:00:31.526 --> 00:00:37.478
The result: up to eighty-three percent fewer
nearest-neighbor searches with no loss in perplexity,

00:00:37.558 --> 00:00:42.280
or up to one point eight five lower perplexity when
the search budget is kept.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:48.323
Retrieval-based language models improve on standard
neural models by fetching nearest-neighbor

00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:52.845
examples from an external datastore and blending
them into the prediction.

00:00:52.925 --> 00:00:57.489
The catch is cost: that datastore search can fire
at every single time step,

00:00:57.569 --> 00:01:00.849
and it is far slower than the model's own forward pass.

00:01:00.929 --> 00:01:06.104
This frequent search is the single most critical
bottleneck that keeps these otherwise

00:01:06.184 --> 00:01:08.670
powerful models out of practical settings.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:13.073
The key observation is that a flat datastore throws
away structure.

00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:15.277
If a retrieved entry was useful now,

00:01:15.357 --> 00:01:19.809
the entry that follows it in the original text is
very likely useful next.

00:01:19.889 --> 00:01:24.585
And entries whose key vectors are close tend to be
followed by the same token.

00:01:24.665 --> 00:01:29.545
Existing approaches like Adaptive Retrieval simply
learn when to skip the search,

00:01:29.625 --> 00:01:36.220
but when they skip they fall back entirely on the
base language model and discard the retrieval distribution,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.710
which hurts most in domains where the base model is weak.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.138
RetoMaton makes two changes to the datastore.

00:01:44.218 --> 00:01:48.265
First,

00:01:48.345 --> 00:01:52.392
it saves a pointer from every entry to the entry
that came right after it in the text.

00:01:52.472 --> 00:01:56.520
Second, it clusters entries with similar key
vectors into states,

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.647
and those states share their outgoing pointers.

00:02:00.727 --> 00:02:04.775
Together these turn the flat datastore into a
weighted finite automaton.

00:02:04.855 --> 00:02:08.903
Building it is completely unsupervised, requires no
extra training data,

00:02:08.982 --> 00:02:13.030
and works whether the automaton is constructed from
the model's own training corpus or from a brand-new domain.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.983
RetoMaton stores each datastore entry as a triple
of key, value, and pointer,

00:02:18.063 --> 00:02:22.213
where the pointer references the entry that
followed it in the corpus.

00:02:22.293 --> 00:02:25.234
Entries with close keys are clustered into states,

00:02:25.314 --> 00:02:28.436
and a state inherits all the pointers of its members.

00:02:28.516 --> 00:02:35.385
At test time the model keeps a small set of active
states and traverses the automaton alongside the language model.

00:02:35.465 --> 00:02:41.186
To move forward it just follows the pointers of
entries whose value matches the generated token,

00:02:41.266 --> 00:02:42.757
which is essentially free.

00:02:42.837 --> 00:02:48.316
A full nearest-neighbor search is only triggered
when the number of valid onward transitions

00:02:48.396 --> 00:02:50.008
drops below a threshold tau.

00:02:50.088 --> 00:02:55.144
The automaton's transition weights are computed
dynamically from the distance between

00:02:55.224 --> 00:02:58.468
the current hidden state and the entries in each state,

00:02:58.548 --> 00:03:03.060
and the resulting distribution is interpolated with
the base language model.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.498
The method is evaluated in two settings.

00:03:07.578 --> 00:03:11.636
For standard in-domain language modeling the
authors use WikiText-103,

00:03:11.716 --> 00:03:15.774
a Wikipedia benchmark with one hundred and three
million training tokens,

00:03:15.854 --> 00:03:19.912
and a two hundred forty seven million parameter
Transformer as the base model,

00:03:19.992 --> 00:03:24.050
producing a datastore of one hundred and three
million entries clustered into one million states.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.188
For domain adaptation they use the law-domain
corpus Law-MT with nineteen million tokens

00:03:28.268 --> 00:03:32.326
and a larger six hundred fifty six million
parameter base model,

00:03:32.406 --> 00:03:36.464
clustered into two hundred thousand states.

00:03:36.544 --> 00:03:40.602
Throughout,

00:03:40.682 --> 00:03:44.740
RetoMaton is compared against the original kNN-LM
and against Adaptive Retrieval.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.184
The results are strong in both regimes.

00:03:49.264 --> 00:03:53.327
On WikiText-103,

00:03:53.407 --> 00:03:57.471
RetoMaton matches the perplexity of kNN-LM while
skipping eighty-one percent of the searches,

00:03:57.551 --> 00:04:01.615
and even when it performs a search at every step it
still lowers perplexity,

00:04:01.695 --> 00:04:05.758
because the carried-over pointers reinforce the
correct neighbors.

00:04:05.838 --> 00:04:09.902
On the Law-MT domain-adaptation task the gains are
larger and much more robust: perplexity

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:14.045
drops from twelve point three four to ten point four nine,

00:04:14.125 --> 00:04:18.189
and as more searches are saved RetoMaton's
perplexity climbs only very gently while plain

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:22.333
kNN-LM's perplexity blows up exponentially.

00:04:22.413 --> 00:04:26.476
Overall the automaton either cuts perplexity by up
to one point eight five,

00:04:26.556 --> 00:04:30.620
or saves up to eighty-three percent of the searches
with no loss.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.743
An ablation teases apart the two ingredients.

00:04:33.823 --> 00:04:36.754
Using pointers alone, with no clustering at all,

00:04:36.834 --> 00:04:42.838
already beats every baseline and matches kNN-LM
while saving more than sixty percent of searches,

00:04:42.918 --> 00:04:47.354
so the pointers deliver most of the benefit when
few searches are saved.

00:04:47.434 --> 00:04:50.553
Clustering contributes mainly at high saving rates,

00:04:50.633 --> 00:04:52.686
from about seventy percent onward,

00:04:52.766 --> 00:04:57.139
where it lets the model stay search-free over
longer stretches of text.

00:04:57.219 --> 00:05:01.718
On cluster count, half a million and one million
means perform similarly,

00:05:01.798 --> 00:05:04.290
while one hundred thousand is too coarse,

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:10.500
and a cheaper greedy clustering wins at zero saved
searches but fades as the saving fraction grows.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.618
The headline numbers are simple to remember.

00:05:13.698 --> 00:05:19.957
RetoMaton saves up to eighty-three percent of
nearest-neighbor searches with no loss in perplexity,

00:05:20.037 --> 00:05:26.425
or alternatively lowers perplexity by as much as
one point eight five when the search budget is kept.

00:05:26.505 --> 00:05:31.548
On WikiText-103 it matches kNN-LM while skipping
eighty-one percent of searches.

00:05:31.628 --> 00:05:38.720
And on a fine-tuned law-domain model it pushes
perplexity from eight point six one down to seven point one zero,

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.050
a relative reduction of more than seventeen percent.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:47.254
The lasting idea is that a retrieval datastore has
structure worth exploiting.

00:05:47.334 --> 00:05:53.100
By linking consecutive entries with pointers and
grouping similar ones into automaton states,

00:05:53.180 --> 00:06:00.204
RetoMaton lets a language model carry retrieval
forward in time instead of searching from scratch at every token.

00:06:00.284 --> 00:06:04.730
It is unsupervised, works with any base model,
transfers across domains,

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.811
and unifies token, chunk, and sequence retrieval,

00:06:07.891 --> 00:06:12.400
all while cutting the dominant cost of
retrieval-based language modeling.
